@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Data & Privacy";
}

<div class="row justify-content-center">
    <div class="col-lg-9 col-xl-8">
        <header class="mb-4">
            <h1>@ViewData["Title"]</h1>
            <p class="lead">
                This page describes how the current Job Application Tracker
                implementation stores and handles data. The project is designed
                primarily for local or self-hosted use.
            </p>
            <div class="bg-body-tertiary border border-start border-4 border-primary rounded-3 p-3 mb-3">
                This is an implementation overview of the current repository,
                not a formal legal policy or regulatory-compliance statement.
            </div>
        </header>

        <section class="mb-5" aria-labelledby="stored-data-heading">
            <h2 id="stored-data-heading" class="h4">
                Data stored by the application
            </h2>
            <p>Job application records may contain:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Company name and position</li>
                <li>Application source and job URL</li>
                <li>Location and application date</li>
                <li>Application status and notes</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Entity Framework Core stores these records in the configured
                SQLite database. Its contents depend on what the user enters.
                When the application starts with an empty job applications
                table, the initializer adds example records. These examples are
                demonstration data and should not be treated as private
                production data.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-5" aria-labelledby="account-data-heading">
            <h2 id="account-data-heading" class="h4">
                Local account data
            </h2>
            <p>The local <code>AppUser</code> record stores:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>A username</li>
                <li>A normalized username used for matching</li>
                <li>A password hash</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Plaintext passwords are not fields on the <code>AppUser</code>
                entity. When the users table is empty, the application creates
                one local user from configured seed credentials and stores a
                hash of that password. This is an educational local
                authentication implementation, not a complete account-management
                system or production identity platform.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-5" aria-labelledby="authentication-heading">
            <h2 id="authentication-heading" class="h4">
                Authentication cookie
            </h2>
            <p>
                After a successful login, the application places a signed JSON
                Web Token (JWT) in an HttpOnly cookie. HttpOnly reduces direct
                access from browser JavaScript, and <code>SameSite=Strict</code>
                limits when the browser sends the cookie across sites. The
                cookie expires at the same time as the token, and its Secure flag
                is enabled when login occurs over HTTPS.
            </p>
            <p>
                Logging out removes the browser cookie. The current application
                does not provide server-side token revocation, and the signed
                token should not be understood as an encrypted cookie or a
                production-grade session system.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-5" aria-labelledby="browser-preferences-heading">
            <h2 id="browser-preferences-heading" class="h4">
                Browser preferences
            </h2>
            <p>
                Mode and palette preferences are stored locally in the browser.
                They are not part of the local user record and are not persisted
                to SQLite, so choices may differ between browsers or devices.
                Clearing browser storage resets the preferences to the configured
                defaults.
            </p>
            <p class="text-body-secondary">
                The storage keys are <code>jobApplicationTracker.theme</code> and
                <code>jobApplicationTracker.palette</code>.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-5" aria-labelledby="local-configuration-heading">
            <h2 id="local-configuration-heading" class="h4">
                Local configuration and secrets
            </h2>
            <p>
                The development setup expects the seed-user credentials and JWT
                signing key to come from .NET User Secrets or another appropriate
                secret source. Secret values should not be committed to the
                repository.
            </p>
            <p>
                Git ignore rules cover SQLite database files and their sidecar
                files to reduce the risk of accidental inclusion in a commit.
                Ignoring those files does not encrypt them. Anyone operating the
                application remains responsible for protecting local database
                files and secrets.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-5" aria-labelledby="external-services-heading">
            <h2 id="external-services-heading" class="h4">
                External services and links
            </h2>
            <p>
                The current repository implementation includes no analytics
                integration or third-party tracking script. Its browser libraries
                are served from local <code>wwwroot</code> files, and its server
                code does not configure an outbound HTTP API client.
            </p>
            <p>
                A saved job URL may take the user to an external website. That
                destination has its own data and privacy practices. Modified or
                separately deployed copies of this project may also add external
                services that are not described here.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-5" aria-labelledby="deployment-heading">
            <h2 id="deployment-heading" class="h4">
                Self-hosted deployment
            </h2>
            <p>Someone deploying a copy controls decisions such as:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>Hosting, HTTPS/TLS, and access control</li>
                <li>Secret management and database access</li>
                <li>Backups, logging, and monitoring</li>
                <li>Any additional integrations</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                Those choices can change how the application handles data.
                Operators are responsible for reviewing their environment and
                determining their own technical and legal obligations.
            </p>
        </section>

        <section class="mb-4" aria-labelledby="limitations-heading">
            <h2 id="limitations-heading" class="h4">
                Current limitations
            </h2>
            <ul>
                <li>The application uses one configured local-user model.</li>
                <li>Registration and password recovery are not implemented.</li>
                <li>
                    Job application records have no per-user ownership
                    relationship; authenticated users would access the same
                    application dataset.
                </li>
                <li>There is no configurable data-retention policy.</li>
                <li>
                    Automated data export and deletion workflows are not
                    implemented.
                </li>
                <li>
                    The project does not implement database encryption at rest.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Local authentication is educational and is not a production
                    identity platform.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </section>
    </div>
</div>
